Michael Lehotay wrote:

> Re-introducing this seems like a plausible idea. But it would make it
> harder to gain levels early in the game, if each gnome was worth less
> experience than the one before it. Maybe it would be better to dish
> out fewer experience points for killing the same individual monster
> more than once (1/2 less each time?). This would reduce the benefits
> of camping on certain monsters.

I like the basic idea behind this. However, I think Moria's system in
this might work better: divide the experience you get by your own EXP
level.

Of course, this is only necessary because of the scoring system. It
would also influence the rate of gaining EXP: you would rise in level
much more slowly. So, the boundaries for gaining levels should also be
changed, if this were to be implemented.

> Another idea I had was to scale the score by the number of turns it
> took to win. The current algorithm has a step that says "The score so
> far is doubled if you ascended." What if we changed that to "if you
> ascended, then score = 2*score * 30000/turns" ? This would reward
> fast games and penalize slow games.

Not wanting to repeat the "Death Drop" discussion, I just want to remakt
that I think slow games shouldn't be penalised. Your stated goal is to
retrieve the Amulet for your god, not to do it as quickly as possible.
Gods usually have plenty of time, so they would be more concerned with
accuracy, not with speed.

